<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-22r1f (P0, reproduced against the DEPLOYED cloud engine 0.1.56).

    TWO measured divergences between service-mode memory search and the SQLite
    FTS5 baseline it replaced, both rooted in the same place — PostgreSQL's
    text-search parser versus FTS5's unicode61 tokenizer. Both are fixed here,
    in ONE regenerated column, because each fix rewrites the same generated
    column and two changesets would rewrite the table twice.

    DIVERGENCE 1 — SEPARATOR TOKENS. PostgreSQL classifies 'auth-design.md' as
    a FILE token and keeps it WHOLE, whereas FTS5 splits on every
    non-alphanumeric and emits 'auth', 'design', 'md'. plainto_tsquery
    ('english','auth') therefore had nothing to match, and a title was
    findable in service mode ONLY by its exact full string, or when it
    contained no separator at all.

        title                       query          sqlite  service
        RDR-025-implementation.md   RDR-025             1        0
        auth-design.md              auth                1        0
        auth-design.md              auth-design         1        0
        auth-design.md              auth-design.md      1        1
        plaintitle                  plaintitle          1        1

    '<name>.md' is the dominant T2 title convention, and SERVICE has been the
    storage default since 6.0 — so this is a silent wrong answer on a
    default-on read path: the search succeeds, returns 200, and reports zero
    hits. Confirmed on a real populated install, not a fresh test tenant:
    memory_search('nonrelocatable') returned nothing while memory_search
    ('dyld') returned the very entry whose TITLE contains 'nonrelocatable'.

    DIVERGENCE 2 — DIACRITIC FOLDING. FTS5's unicode61 folds diacritics, so
    'resume' finds 'résumé'; to_tsvector('english', ...) does not. Measured on
    the content column with no title involvement, so unlike the rows above
    this one is not a tokenization artifact:

        content                     query          sqlite  service
        résumé cafetière naïve      resume              1        0

    THIS IS THE SECOND FALSIFICATION OF THE SAME PARITY CLAIM. catalog-015
    fixed the identical separator bug on catalog_documents in 2026-07-13
    (nexus-8gue1, GH #1397) and its comment already recorded that "the
    catalog-001 'PG >= FTS5 superset' claim was false for filenames".
    memory-001 makes the same superset claim (docs/rdr/rdr-152-fts-parity-
    contract.md Store 1) and it is false for exactly the same reason, plus
    diacritics. The separator segment below is deliberately a line-for-line
    application of the catalog-015 shape so the two tables stay reasoned about
    together.

    NOT A DIVERGENCE, despite what the bead first recorded: the trailing-*
    prefix operator. The CLIENT's own sanitizer quotes it ('*' is in
    _sanitize_fts5's _FTS5_SPECIAL), and a star inside quotes is not FTS5's
    prefix operator — 'auth*' reaches SQLite as the phrase "auth*" and matches
    the CONTENT column 0 times there too. The SQLite arm's apparent hit came
    entirely from the fixture's TITLE 'auth.md' tokenizing to 'auth'. The
    separator segment below reproduces that title hit on the service side, so
    that parity row closes here with no query-side parser and no change to
    plainto_tsquery's injection safety.

    ── WHY translate() AND NOT THE unaccent EXTENSION ────────────────────────

    unaccent is the obvious reach for diacritic folding and it is the WRONG
    tool here, on two independently sufficient grounds. Both were measured
    AFTER the unaccent version had already been written and passed the full
    Java suite — the testcontainer image (pgvector/pgvector:pg17) carries the
    full contrib set, so nothing in mvn could see either problem.

    1. IT BRICKS EVERY EXISTING INSTALL. The shipped PG bundle is built by
       scripts/build_pg_bundle.sh, which installs exactly contrib/pg_trgm.
       Checked against the actual published artifacts:

           engine-service-v0.1.52  pg_trgm.control plpgsql.control vector.control
           engine-service-v0.1.56  pg_trgm.control plpgsql.control vector.control

       So CREATE EXTENSION unaccent aborts Liquibase at engine boot and the
       service never binds. Caught by the pytest engine arm, which boots the
       real bundle rather than a container image:
           "Failed SQL: (0) CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS unaccent ...
            must first be installed on the system where PostgreSQL is running"
       Adding contrib/unaccent to the bundle is possible, but it couples this
       changeset to a rebuilt-and-reseeded PG bundle on every platform and
       makes any ordering slip an engine that will not start.

    2. IT OVERSHOOTS THE PARITY TARGET. FTS5's default remove_diacritics=1
       folds the Latin-1 range ONLY. Measured against the FTS5 baseline this
       column is supposed to match:

           query      finds        what it is
           resume     résumé    ✓  Latin-1
           cafetiere  cafetière ✓  Latin-1
           naive      naïve     ✓  Latin-1
           angstrom   Ångström  ✓  Latin-1
           strasse    Straße    ✗  no ß expansion
           lodz       Łódź      ✗  Latin Extended-A
           zolw       żółw      ✗  Latin Extended-A
           ellada     Ελλάδα    ✗  Greek
           privet     привет    ✗  Cyrillic

       unaccent folds all nine. Using it would make service BROADER than the
       baseline in a parity contract whose entire purpose is to make the two
       agree — trading a missing-results divergence for an extra-results one.

    fold_diacritics below is a 1:1 translate() over exactly the Latin-1
    supplement letters, which reproduces the measured FTS5 column above
    row-for-row: Latin-1 folded, ß / Latin-Ext-A / Greek / Cyrillic untouched.
    It is genuinely IMMUTABLE (translate is), so unlike an unaccent wrapper it
    needs no deliberate misstatement to the planner — unaccent is only STABLE
    (BOTH overloads in PG17, including the two-arg regdictionary form widely
    documented as immutable), and a generated column referencing it is
    rejected outright with "generation expression is not immutable".

    Deliberately NOT handling the expanding folds (ß->ss, æ->ae): translate is
    1:1 by construction, and FTS5 does not expand them either, so matching its
    behaviour and matching translate's capability happen to coincide.

    ── DESIGN: REPLACE, DO NOT ADD ───────────────────────────────────────────

    Diacritic folding wraps the EXISTING segments rather than adding a fifth.
    Replace-not-add is index-size-neutral, and it cannot narrow anything: the
    fold is a deterministic mapping applied identically to the stored side and
    the query side (MemoryRepository.ftsQuery), so any query that matched
    before still matches. An added segment would instead have doubled the
    stored lexemes for title and content.

    The separator segment IS additive, because it is a genuinely different
    projection of the same text — weight 'A' matches the title segment it
    augments, so a title-fragment hit ranks like a title hit, not below
    content. Existing segments are untouched by it (superset preserved).

    SCOPE of the separator segment, deliberately narrow: TITLE only. content
    is prose and already tokenizes correctly; tags already use 'simple'. The
    measured defect is the title column, and widening the STORED expression
    beyond the evidence would grow the index for no demonstrated gain.
    Folding, by contrast, is measured on CONTENT, so it covers every segment.

    ── MECHANICS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    PG cannot ALTER a generated column's expression: DROP + re-ADD (table
    rewrite) + recreate the GIN index. ACCESS EXCLUSIVE for the rewrite is
    fine in the single-instance rm-then-run deploy window (nothing serves
    during boot — same framing as catalog-014/015); Liquibase runs the
    changeset in one transaction, so an interrupted rewrite rolls back whole,
    never a dropped-column limbo. DDL only, no row DML, so the FORCE-RLS
    silent-no-op class (nexus-1wjmq) does not apply.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="memory-002-0" author="nexus-22r1f">
        <comment>fold_diacritics: Latin-1 folding matching FTS5 unicode61 remove_diacritics=1, as pure IMMUTABLE translate() so no contrib extension is needed at boot</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true">
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nexus.fold_diacritics(text) RETURNS text
    LANGUAGE sql IMMUTABLE STRICT PARALLEL SAFE AS
    $$ SELECT translate($1,
         'ÀÁÂÃÄÅÇÈÉÊËÌÍÎÏÑÒÓÔÕÖØÙÚÛÜÝàáâãäåçèéêëìíîïñòóôõöøùúûüýÿ',
         'AAAAAACEEEEIIIINOOOOOOUUUUYaaaaaaceeeeiiiinoooooouuuuyy') $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true">
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS nexus.fold_diacritics(text);
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="memory-002-1" author="nexus-22r1f">
        <comment>fts_vector: separator-normalized title segment (auth-design.md findable by auth) + diacritic folding on every segment (resume finds résumé)</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true">
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS nexus.idx_memory_fts;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory DROP COLUMN fts_vector;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory
    ADD COLUMN fts_vector TSVECTOR GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
        setweight(to_tsvector('english',
            nexus.fold_diacritics(coalesce(title,   ''))), 'A') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('english',
            nexus.fold_diacritics(coalesce(content, ''))), 'B') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('simple',
            nexus.fold_diacritics(coalesce(tags,    ''))), 'C') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('simple',
            nexus.fold_diacritics(
                translate(coalesce(title, ''), '/.-_', '    '))), 'A')
    ) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_memory_fts ON nexus.memory USING GIN (fts_vector);
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true">
DROP INDEX IF EXISTS nexus.idx_memory_fts;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory DROP COLUMN fts_vector;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory
    ADD COLUMN fts_vector TSVECTOR GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
        setweight(to_tsvector('english', coalesce(title,   '')), 'A') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('english', coalesce(content, '')), 'B') ||
        setweight(to_tsvector('simple',  coalesce(tags,    '')), 'C')
    ) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_memory_fts ON nexus.memory USING GIN (fts_vector);
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
